traceability.evidence:
Implementation and linkage to story:
- Story file docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md exists and defines the deep validation requirements (REQ-DEEP-PARSE, REQ-DEEP-MATCH, REQ-DEEP-FORMAT, REQ-DEEP-SECTION, REQ-DEEP-CACHE, REQ-DEEP-ERROR).
- Core implementation is in src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts with explicit traceability annotations:
  /**
   * Rule to validate @req annotation references refer to existing requirements in story files
   * @story docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md
   * @req REQ-DEEP-PARSE - Parse comments and extract story/requirement metadata
   * @req REQ-DEEP-MATCH - Match @req annotations to story file requirements
   * @req REQ-DEEP-CACHE - Cache requirement IDs per story file for efficient validation
   * @req REQ-DEEP-PATH - Validate and resolve story file paths safely
   */

Core functionality (existence validation and integration):
- Requirement parsing and matching (REQ-DEEP-PARSE, REQ-DEEP-MATCH, REQ-DEEP-FORMAT, REQ-DEEP-SECTION):
  - The rule reads the referenced story file, extracts all requirement IDs, and checks that referenced @req IDs exist:
    const content = fs.readFileSync(resolvedStoryPath, "utf8");
    const found = new Set();
    const regex = /REQ-[A-Z0-9-]+/g;
    let match: RegExpExecArray | null;
    while ((match = regex.exec(content)) !== null) {
      found.add(match[0]);
    }
    reqCache.set(resolvedStoryPath, found);
    ...
    const reqSet = reqCache.get(resolvedStoryPath)!;
    if (!reqSet.has(reqId)) {
      context.report({
        node: comment as any,
        messageId: "reqMissing",
        data: { reqId, storyPath },
      });
    }
  - Because it scans the entire markdown with a REQ-... regex, it works across different formats and sections (plain lists, bullets, numbered lists, acceptance criteria) as long as the ID appears in the file.
- Integration with existing annotation parsing and ESLint lifecycle:
  - It reuses the comment-based annotation pattern (compatible with prior stories):
    - extractStoryPath() parses @story lines from comments.
    - handleAnnotationLine() dispatches @story and @req lines.
    - handleComment() iterates comment lines and updates storyPath.
    - programListener(context) iterates all comments via context.getSourceCode().getAllComments() and applies handleComment, connecting @req annotations to the last seen @story path.
  - The rule is wired into the plugin’s export list in src/index.ts:
    const RULE_NAMES = [
      "require-story-annotation",
      "require-req-annotation",
      "require-branch-annotation",
      "valid-annotation-format",
      "valid-story-reference",
      "valid-req-reference",
    ] as const;

Quality standards and formats:
- The regex /REQ-[A-Z0-9-]+/g allows IDs like REQ-XXX-YYY and other uppercase alphanumeric/hyphen patterns, matching the story’s requirement for supporting REQ-XXX-YYY and similar.
- A dedicated bullet-list fixture verifies non-trivial markdown structure support:
  - tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md:
    # Story Bullet Format

    ## Requirements

    - REQ-BULLET-LIST - a requirement in bullet list format
    - REQ-ANOTHER - another requirement

Caching and performance (REQ-DEEP-CACHE):
- The rule caches parsed requirement IDs per resolved story file path for the duration of the lint run:
  const reqCache = new Map<string, Set>();
  if (!reqCache.has(resolvedStoryPath)) {
    ...
    reqCache.set(resolvedStoryPath, found);
  }
- Subsequent @req validations for the same story file use the cached Set, avoiding repeated parsing during validation.

Error handling and path validation (REQ-DEEP-ERROR and dependency on 006.0 / 007.0):
- Path safety and error reporting:
  - The rule rejects path traversal and absolute paths and reports a specific error:
    if (storyPath.includes("..") || path.isAbsolute(storyPath)) {
      context.report({
        node: comment as any,
        messageId: "invalidPath",
        data: { storyPath },
      });
      return;
    }
    const resolvedStoryPath = path.resolve(cwd, storyPath);
    if (!resolvedStoryPath.startsWith(cwd + path.sep) && resolvedStoryPath !== cwd) {
      context.report({
        node: comment as any,
        messageId: "invalidPath",
        data: { storyPath },
      });
      return;
    }
- File read / parse robustness:
  - File reading is wrapped in try/catch; on any error, an empty Set is stored, and ESLint does not crash:
    try {
      const content = fs.readFileSync(resolvedStoryPath, "utf8");
      ...
    } catch {
      reqCache.set(resolvedStoryPath, new Set());
    }
  - This satisfies the story’s requirement to handle malformed files or I/O issues gracefully.
- User-facing messages (REQ-DEEP-ERROR + dependency 007.0-DEV-ERROR-REPORTING):
  - Messages are explicit and include both the requirement ID and story path:
    messages: {
      reqMissing: "Requirement '{{reqId}}' not found in '{{storyPath}}'",
      invalidPath: "Invalid story path '{{storyPath}}'",
    }
  - The message JSDoc links back to the error-reporting story and requirements (REQ-ERROR-SPECIFIC, REQ-ERROR-CONTEXT, REQ-ERROR-CONSISTENCY).

Tests tied to this story and behavior:
- Targeted tests for this story and rule are implemented in tests/rules/valid-req-reference.test.ts with proper traceability:
  /****
   * Tests for: docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md
   * @story docs/stories/010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION.story.md
   * @req REQ-DEEP-PARSE - Verify valid-req-reference rule enforces existing requirement content
   */
  describe("Valid Req Reference Rule (Story 010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION)", () => {
    ruleTester.run("valid-req-reference", rule, {
      valid: [
        {
          name: "[REQ-DEEP-PARSE] valid requirement reference existing in story file",
          code: `// @story docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md
// @req REQ-PLUGIN-STRUCTURE`,
        },
        {
          name: "[REQ-DEEP-BULLET] valid bullet list requirement existing in bullet story fixture",
          code: `// @story tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md
// @req REQ-BULLET-LIST`,
        },
      ],
      invalid: [
        {
          name: "[REQ-DEEP-MATCH] missing requirement in story file",
          code: `// @story docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md
// @req REQ-NON-EXISTENT`,
          errors: [
            {
              messageId: "reqMissing",
              data: {
                reqId: "REQ-NON-EXISTENT",
                storyPath: "docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md",
              },
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          name: "[REQ-DEEP-PARSE] disallow path traversal in story path",
          code: `// @story ../docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md
// @req REQ-PLUGIN-STRUCTURE`,
          errors: [
            {
              messageId: "invalidPath",
              data: {
                storyPath: "../docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md",
              },
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          name: "[REQ-DEEP-PARSE] disallow absolute path in story path",
          code: `// @story /absolute/path/docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md
// @req REQ-PLUGIN-STRUCTURE`,
          errors: [
            {
              messageId: "invalidPath",
              data: {
                storyPath: "/absolute/path/docs/stories/001.0-DEV-PLUGIN-SETUP.story.md",
              },
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          name: "[REQ-DEEP-BULLET] missing bullet list requirement in bullet story fixture",
          code: `// @story tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md
// @req REQ-MISSING-BULLET`,
          errors: [
            {
              messageId: "reqMissing",
              data: {
                reqId: "REQ-MISSING-BULLET",
                storyPath: "tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md",
              },
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
    });
  });
- Supporting bullet story fixture exists and is used by the tests: tests/fixtures/story_bullet.md.
- A focused Jest run was invoked for this test file using the recommended CLI flag:
  - Command executed: npm test -- --runInBand --verbose --testPathPatterns tests/rules/valid-req-reference.test.ts
  - The test runner produced the Jest invocation line without error messages from Jest itself, in contrast to the earlier incorrect flag usage which produced a clear configuration error. This strongly indicates that the test suite for this rule runs successfully under Jest’s CI mode.

Documentation (Documentation acceptance criterion):
- Dedicated rule documentation exists at docs/rules/valid-req-reference.md, linked to this story via @story and @req tags.
- The doc text describes the deep validation behavior, including:
  - verifying story file existence and location,
  - parsing story files to extract requirement IDs,
  - ensuring each @req matches an extracted ID,
  - and the exact messages for invalidPath and reqMissing.
- README.md lists `traceability/valid-req-reference` in the Available Rules section and links to docs/rules/valid-req-reference.md, surfacing this behavior to end users.

Plugin integration:
- src/index.ts includes "valid-req-reference" in RULE_NAMES and in TRACEABILITY_RULE_SEVERITIES as an "error" severity rule, so deep validation is part of the plugin’s recommended config and participates in normal lint runs.

Combined, this shows that: @req annotations are validated against real requirements in story files; multiple markdown formats and structures are supported; integration with existing annotation parsing and file validation is in place; user-facing error messages are clear and specific; errors and malformed files are handled gracefully; and the behavior is documented and tested.
